        <Image
          format="png"
          src={facultyEvalRecommendations}
          alt="Tutor evaluation dashboard — key findings, overview, and suggestions & recommendations"
          width={3000}
          quality={100}
          class="rounded-xl w-full h-auto object-contain"
        />
      </figure>

      <div class="cs-meta cs-wide">
        <MetaItem label="Project type">
          <p class="cs-inline-result">Capstone project, AI evaluation</p>
        </MetaItem>
        <MetaItem label="My role">
          <p class="cs-inline-result">UX Designer</p>
        </MetaItem>
        <MetaItem label="Tools">
          <Chip title="Figma" level="professional" showLevel={false}>
            <Icon slot="icon" name="figma-brands-solid-full" size={18} aria-hidden />
          </Chip>
          <Chip title="Claude" level="professional" showLevel={false}>
            <Icon slot="icon" name="claude-brands-solid-full" size={18} aria-hidden />
          </Chip>
        </MetaItem>
      </div>

      <StatRow>
        <Stat value="4 mo" label="project timeline" />
        <Stat value="4" label="users in moderated prototype testing" />
      </StatRow>

      <CaseStudyQuestion question="How can faculty trust an AI tutor before students ever see it?">
        <p class="project-description">
          Faculty can configure AI tutors easily, but had no reliable way to test
          quality, misconceptions, or pedagogy before students used one, and no
          onboarding to learn the evaluation tool.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyQuestion>

      <SectionHeading index="01" title="Goal" id="capstone-goal" />
      <div class="cs-section-body">
        <p class="project-description">
          Faculty could publish a tutor, but had no repeatable way to test
          how it would actually teach.
        </p>
        <Points numbered={false}>
          <Point title="How might we?">
            <p>
              Help faculty assess and refine a tutor before it goes live, with
              clear evidence, explainable ratings, and updates they can approve?
            </p>
          </Point>
          <Point title="Design goal">
            <p>
              Pair simple onboarding for each configuration field with a
              dashboard that moves from high-level warnings to granular checks.
            </p>
          </Point>
        </Points>
      </div>

      <SectionHeading
        index="02"
        title="Research & evaluation framework"
        id="capstone-research"
      />
      <div class="cs-section-body">
        <p class="project-description">
          I paired secondary research with past UT Sage interview data to find
          configuration gaps, then turned AI evaluation benchmarks into a scorecard
          instructors can read.
        </p>
        <Points numbered={false}>
          <Point title="8 evaluation dimensions: response quality">
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5">
              <li><strong>D1 Instruction-following:</strong> honors faculty instructions.</li>
              <li><strong>D2 Truthfulness:</strong> accurate, no hallucinated content.</li>
              <li><strong>D3 Style / tone:</strong> warm yet academically rigorous.</li>
              <li><strong>D4 Visual reasoning:</strong> interprets diagrams and graphs.</li>
              <li><strong>D5 Visual perception:</strong> reads visual inputs from learners.</li>
              <li><strong>D6 Calibration:</strong> pacing fits the learner.</li>
              <li><strong>D7 Conciseness:</strong> replies stay scoped.</li>
              <li><strong>D8 Emotional responsiveness:</strong> handles frustration constructively.</li>
            </ul>
          </Point>
          <Point title="7 tutoring skills: instructional behaviors">
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5">
              <li><strong>T1</strong> Identifies misconceptions explicitly.</li>
              <li><strong>T2</strong> Guides with questions, not answers.</li>
              <li><strong>T3</strong> Uses examples and analogies.</li>
              <li><strong>T4</strong> Offers alternate solution paths.</li>
              <li><strong>T5</strong> Scaffolds step-by-step when problems are dense.</li>
              <li><strong>T6</strong> Recalls syllabus or uploaded resources.</li>
              <li><strong>T7</strong> Judges correct and incorrect steps accurately.</li>
            </ul>
          </Point>
        </Points>
      </div>

      <SectionHeading
        index="03"
        title="Prototyping"
        id="capstone-prototyping"
      />
      <div class="cs-section-body">
        <p class="project-description">
          Two surfaces, designed together in Figma: tutorial overlays explaining
          Create, Configure, and Train, and an evaluation view that leads with key
          findings.
        </p>
        <div
